To create a video with autoplay, loop, and mute functionality in HTML, you can use the `<video>` element along with some CSS styles to control its behavior. Here's how you can achieve this:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Autoplaying Looping Muted Video</title>
    <style>
        /* Add these styles to ensure the video is centered */
        body {
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
            background-color: #f0f0f0;
        }

        .video-container {
            position: relative;
            width: 500px;
            height: 500px;
        }

        .video-container iframe {
            position: absolute;
            top: 0;
            left: 0;
            width: 100%;
            height: 100%;
            border: none;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="video-container">
    <iframe src="your-video-url.mp4"></iframe>
</div>

</body>
</html>
```

### Explanation:
1. **HTML Structure**:
   - The `video` element is used to embed the video.
   - The `autoplay`, `muted`, and `loop` attributes enable autoplay, muting, and looping of the video.

2. **CSS Styling**:
   - The `.video-container` class centers the video within the container.
   - The `iframe` inside the container ensures that the video takes up the full dimensions of the container.

3. **Video Source**:
   - Replace `"your-video-url.mp4"` with the actual URL of your video file.

This setup will automatically play the video when the page loads, loop it continuously, and keep it muted. The video will be centered on the screen using Flexbox layout.

